{
  "book": {
    "title": "The Broken Crown",
    "author": "Jesse Karjalainen",
    "emoji": "👑",
    "subtitle": "A Legacy Unearthed, A Truth Reclaimed",
    "description": "When Andrew Keynes discovers a buried royal artefact in rural France, he unearths not only a relic but a tangle of lies that spans generations. The find catapults him into a maelstrom of political scandal, personal loss, and unresolved guilt following his daughter’s death. As media frenzy and institutional secrecy close in, he must decide how much of the truth is worth revealing—and what it will cost him to do so. Blending emotional introspection with historical intrigue, *The Broken Crown* is a meditation on grief, integrity, and the heavy inheritance of history.",
    "coverImage": "the_broken_crown_cover.png"
  },
  "audienceMatch": {
    "title": "Audience Insight and Appeal",
    "emoji": "📚",
    "bisac": {
      "primary": "FICTION / Historical / General",
      "secondary": "FICTION / Literary / Family Life",
      "additional": "FICTION / Mystery & Detective / Historical"
    },
    "engagement": "High engagement potential among culturally literate readers aged 35–65 with interest in historical fiction and moral mystery.",
    "keyCharacteristics": "The readership is thoughtful, well-educated, and emotionally engaged—drawn to intelligent fiction that rewards curiosity and empathy. They seek novels that merge historical resonance with personal stakes and subtle tension over spectacle.",
    "detailedAnalysis": "Analysis indicates a strong overlap between *The Broken Crown* and the tastes of readers who appreciate literary suspense over conventional thrillers. Its layered emotional realism, moral ambiguity, and European settings position it to capture both traditional historical fiction readers and those seeking introspective narratives exploring grief and legacy. Engagement drivers include its authenticity of tone, its accessible intellectualism, and its emotional depth across generations."
  },
  "analysisOverview": {
    "title": "Analytical Overview",
    "emoji": "🧭",
    "readership": "The book appeals to reflective readers who value history as a living moral landscape, not a static archive. It connects emotionally with those who appreciate literary fiction with the pulse of a mystery—combining empathy with intellect.",
    "classification": "*The Broken Crown* occupies a refined space between literary and commercial historical fiction. Using a bespoke subgenre taxonomy, it sits at the confluence of ‘Archaeological Adventure’, ‘Aristocratic Decline Fiction’, and ‘Cold Case Mystery’. Its appeal lies in marrying the introspection of *The Remains of the Day* with the intrigue of *The Da Vinci Code*, offering a ‘thinking reader’s’ treasure-hunt novel anchored in realism.",
    "personas": "The report identifies two archetypal readers. Natalie Brennan—the Literary Explorer—values emotional intelligence and philosophical nuance; her engagement signals deep resonance with the novel’s reflective tone. Jonas Holloway—the Thriller Historian—represents the legacy-driven, historically curious reader who seeks moral complexity and narrative payoff. Together, they capture the novel’s dual strength: intellect and intrigue.",
    "marketingGuidance": "Marketing insights position *The Broken Crown* as an emotionally intelligent historical mystery capable of global reach. Campaign strategies should emphasise the contrast between secrecy and revelation, history and humanity. This early-stage marketing report provides a foundation for tone, copy, and targeting; future versions will expand into a standalone product including adaptive campaign assets and advanced audience modelling."
  },
